  "cause": "Chancellor **John Healey** delivers his first Budget this afternoon under Prime Minister **Andy Burnham**, with borrowing costs at historic highs and a package pre-briefed as \"everyday fixes\" (Reuters, FT, Guardian). The headline measure, released this morning, is that **standing charges will be scrapped from household gas bills for about 23 million homes from next year — a fixed charge of roughly £108 a year** (Guardian, Bloomberg, Independent, Reuters). It lands exactly where this page said the Budget's only structural lever was: not the size of the bill but its *shape* — the distribution of network costs between a fixed daily charge and a unit rate. **The catch is already in the coverage**: because the fixed cost does not disappear, it moves into the unit rate, so The Sun's assessment is that \"millions will still pay MORE\" — the households that use the least gas gain most, and heavy users, including the poorly insulated and the elderly, pay the difference (The Sun, This is Money). Alongside it: **adult minimum wage up 3.9%**, a reversal of the cuts to cash ISA allowances (FT), and fuel duty frozen with the 5p discount continued (BBC). **The Budget's decisive number is still the one nobody votes on.** The context is not the £108 but a debt-interest line set by gilt yields at multi-decade highs, in a parliament where the state already takes about £200 a month per household in energy and council tax (BBC, Reuters, Bloomberg). Moving a fixed charge into a unit rate is a redistribution *within* the bill — it does not reduce the total, and it does not touch the interest the Treasury pays on the debt that funds the energy system's transition. Healey has attributed the cost-of-living pressure to the wars in Iran and Ukraine, which is accurate about the transmission and also means no domestic measure can be credited when prices fall (BBC).",
